Under the Swiss Data Protection Act, you have a statutory right of access. You can find out what information a company holds about you by submitting an access request directly to the company (the data controller) asking whether personal data concerning you is being processed [1].

### What information must be provided?
The company must provide the information necessary for you to assert your rights and ensure transparent data processing [2]. In all cases, this includes:
- The processed personal data itself;
- The purpose of the processing;
- The retention period (or criteria used to determine it);
- Available details on the origin of the data if not collected directly from you;
- The controller's contact information and the recipients or categories of recipients if the data has been disclosed [2].

### Does the company have to answer?
Yes. The controller is obligated to respond, as a rule within 30 days [3] and free of charge [4] (exceptions to the fee rule may apply if the effort is disproportionate) [4].

### Can the company refuse?
A private controller may refuse, restrict, or defer access only on specific grounds—such as when a formal law requires it (e.g. professional secrecy), when overriding interests of third parties require it, or if your request is manifestly unfounded or vexatious [5]. If the company refuses, restricts, or delays access, it must state the reasons [6].
